+Self-hosted hardware CI runner guidance
+
+Overview:
+- Many hardware SDKs and device drivers cannot be installed in public CI runners. Use dedicated, secured self-hosted runners for:
+  - Building vendor-specific container images (Neuron, Poplar, SynapseAI).
+  - Running hardware smoke tests (ONNX/TensorRT/Neurons) against attached devices.
+
+Security & operations:
+- Runners should be in a secured VPC or isolated lab network.
+- Limit runner permissions and access; restrict who can trigger hardware builds.
+- Use ephemeral credentials and store secrets in Vault; record evidence manifests when images are promoted.
+
+Example workflow:
+1. Developer pushes tag to branch guarded by CODEOWNERS.
+2. Hardware CI triggers on protected tag; job runs on self-hosted runner with appropriate labels (e.g., "self-hosted, gpu, neuron").
+3. Runner builds image, runs smoke tests, and if successful, pushes to internal registry and records evidence manifest (cosign sign + Rekor).
+
